#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079292} :activity/id #uuid "d2b403a1-8031-5a79-8235-5b648d030a5b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048338 :topic/id #uuid "96be3b68-ea0a-594d-ac9b-2bd958c09c72" :topic/math-academy-id 669} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079292 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079728 :tutorial/content "Suppose that the fourth term of an arithmetic sequence is $6$ and the fifth term is $12.$ Can we use this information to compute the common difference of the sequence?\n\nThe first thing to note is that the sequence is *increasing*, and therefore we expect the common difference $d$ to be positive.\n\nNow, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/6b/6b82ba58c986aa32635e2ebbd8b1e0fd0affdbc81e35a015960777fa72d5f72b.png)\n\nBecause $a_4 = 6$ and $a_5 = 12$ are **consecutive terms**, meaning that $a_5$ comes straight after $a_4$ in the sequence, we can easily compute the common difference $d$ by subtracting $a_4$ from $a_5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{5}-{a}_{4} \\\\  & =12-6 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e69a6b27-01d4-5ee2-81c3-d7d8f834b087" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8911 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d8a0caf7-c1c8-5350-9d6b-8db3c51bdd00" :step/math-academy-id 148381 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079293}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079293 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078423 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078183 :question/id #uuid "cb23119e-7625-5dad-873f-03049677618c" :question/math-academy-id "e-14385"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9307a5e-2eea-5cea-b5f3-2ce6df53961e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049579}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519529} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519575}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Difference Given Two Consecutive Terms"} :step/id #uuid "ca7d2f7b-9202-580e-bad9-b64525e020c2" :step/math-academy-id 148382 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079294}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079294 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079729 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we're given the second and fifth terms only of an arithmetic sequence. For example, suppose that we know that\n\n$$\na_2 = {\\color{red}8}, \\quad a_5 = {\\color{red}23}\n$$\n\nThese terms are not consecutive, and so how can we figure out the common difference $d?$\n\nFirst, let's take a look at the jumps:\n\n![](images/ff/ff83c75935acca7ddd5c421cd81fad85685c68fd782d1d832c068237c751753d.png)\n\nWe see that between ${\\color{red}8}$ and ${\\color{red}23}$, there are a total of ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps. So, to find the value of a single jump, we can take the total difference and divide it by the number of jumps:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{{\\color{red}23}\\,-\\,{\\color{red}8}}{\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{15}{3} = 5\n$$\n\nWe can check that our result $d=5$ is correct by using it to figure out the intermediate terms. Indeed, the common difference $d=5$ gets us from $a_2={\\color{red}8}$ to $a_5={\\color{red}23}$ in ${\\color{blue}3}$ jumps:\n\n![](images/7a/7aa192675aeb3c7be78617035b74362b38974e8501d95c0692238811ae4769f1.png)\n\nIn general, if we're given two terms $a_n$ and $a_m$ of an arithmetic sequence, then we can calculate the common difference using the following formula:\n\n$$\nd = \\frac{a_n-a_m}{n-m}\n$$\n\nUsing this formula to calculate the common difference between $a_{2}=8$ and $a_{5}=23,$ we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{5}-{a}_{2}}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{23-8}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{3} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "dab8ebfb-480a-5a36-9a12-169a69cd8316" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8913 :tutorial/title "Non-Consecutive Terms"} :step/id #uuid "59dd0405-0ca2-5664-bbd2-78d0a305318e" :step/math-academy-id 148386 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079295}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079295 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078424 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078185 :question/id #uuid "a22637f2-14e5-55ff-a41b-1f2a7e0eaa73" :question/math-academy-id "e-14390"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d15f023-4061-51ad-b307-013bd06d3ec1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048938}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078788} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519596} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519606}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Common Difference Given Two Non-Consecutive Terms"} :step/id #uuid "2f2df9f7-ad2a-5436-aa72-25df975ccb0b" :step/math-academy-id 148387}] :activity/title "Finding the Common Difference of an Arithmetic Sequence" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078685}]}
